  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"PO Automation Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is purchase order automation?\", \"a\": \"Purchase order automation is software that runs the buying steps without email chains or spreadsheets. A request is checked against budget and routed to the right approvers. The approved order goes to the supplier as a numbered PO, and deliveries and invoices are checked against it.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I build an automated purchase order system?\", \"a\": \"Write your approval limits and PO numbering down first, then describe them to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-automation-builder'>AI automation builder</a>. It builds the request form, the routing by amount, and the step that issues each numbered PO. Connect your accounting system, then replay a month of past purchases and check every route before staff rely on it.\"}, {\"q\": \"How does an automated purchase order system work?\", \"a\": \"An automated purchase order system creates the PO from data it already holds. Once the last approver signs, the order gets the next PO number, and the supplier, items, and agreed prices are copied onto your template. Each receipt and invoice later posts against that number, and the open balance updates with it.\"}, {\"q\": \"What does a PO approval workflow look like?\", \"a\": \"A typical PO approval workflow routes by amount. Small requests need only the manager, larger ones add finance, and the largest go to the CFO. Category rules can add IT or legal at any amount. For the general pattern, see approval workflow.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are the steps in a purchase order workflow?\", \"a\": \"A typical purchase order workflow has six steps: requisition, approval, PO issue, supplier confirmation, receiving, and invoice matching before payment. The approval step is where your policy lives, meaning who may spend how much and on what. Receiving matters more than it looks, because without a receipt there is nothing to match the invoice against.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you speed up a purchase order approval workflow?\", \"a\": \"Set a dollar amount below which one approver is enough, and send a reminder when anyone holds a request for two days. Let approvers decide in Slack or email instead of a portal they rarely open. In an app built with Emergent, every approver is included, with no per-seat charge for adding one.\"}, {\"q\": \"How much does purchase order automation software cost?\", \"a\": \"Cost follows each vendor's pricing model: per user, per invoice, or a quote based on volume. The table above shows how three AP platforms charge. Emergent is free to start, and the free tier covers building and publishing your app before you pay for anything.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can automated purchase orders be triggered by low stock?\", \"a\": \"Yes. A nightly check compares each item's count with its reorder point, the stock level at which a new order should be placed. When an item drops below that level, the app drafts an automated purchase order with the usual quantity and supplier. A buyer checks the draft before it is sent.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is there a purchase order automation template?\", \"a\": \"Templates give you a PO layout but not your rules. With Emergent you describe your purchase order workflow in plain sentences, with your limits and approvers. The app follows those rules from the first request. Any purchase order approval workflow it builds changes by prompting when the policy does.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can purchase order automation software match invoices?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Two-way matching compares the supplier invoice with the PO before anyone pays it. Three-way matching adds the receiving record, which stops payment for goods that never arrived. The check usually opens the <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-automation-builder/accounts-payable-automation'>accounts payable automation</a> stage, once the bill comes in.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
